[Music]  i want to share with you all three  versions of an exercise that i recently  came up with going from beginner  intermediate  [Music]  and advanced  i quite like this exercise because it  has a blend of a sort of mysterious  sound and also a heroic quality and when  i'm practicing this i also get to work a  little more on my left hand than my  right hand which i always find is a  great thing because by default i notice  myself over practicing the right hand  and not paying attention to the left  hand  all of these exercises are alternating  between minor and major triads i'll be  going more into the details behind each  exercise after the demonstrations so  let's first go into each exercise  demonstrated in six different  transpositions  [Music]  for those of you interested in the  theory behind the chord progression it's  relatively simple we're first starting  in a minor  with a minor one chord so this is an a  minor triad  going to  a major five  so this is  e major  and here is what's slightly different  instead of playing a  major three chord which would be the  most common thing  i'm changing that to  a minor three so c minor  and  then i'm going to a g major chord you  can consider it a major seven chord  within a minor still  or you can look at it as the five chord  of the c minor chord  or you can also look at it as a minor  four  which is slightly altered going to  a major one  the last major triad  turns into a minor triad  and this is the beginning of the next  transposition  for version one i first recommend  playing all of these three notes of each  triad together first  hand separately then break it up so  you're first playing the bottom note  then the middle note  then the top note  same for the next chord  [Music]  take note of the fact that  after the first triad we're repeating  that top note  going from the thumb to the fifth finger  [Music]  and to get that to be smooth you really  don't need to  do much other than bring these two  fingers together  so bring it together  instead of  trying to  switch over if that makes sense  [Music]  after this first iteration  [Music]  the second version uses four notes in  the left hand per chord instead of three  here are the chords a minor  [Music]  e major  [Music]  c minor  [Music]  g major  so it is basically a triad  plus an octave on top  or surrounding it  and this is broken up  like so  so this third  note here  which is the top of the triad  is repeated  [Music]  now something that i noticed just now is  that in the right hand i tend to use  mostly 5432 i don't  [Music]  always end with a thumb another  variation that would help you practice  and learn this  is to break it up  into two chords  [Music]  so there are a lot of variations that  you can play around with  [Music]  in version 3 i am expanding each chord  so that it is much wider  i might get used to that pattern first  and then just roll the tenths  [Music]  and then work on those repeated notes  i would just stop after the repeat so  after one two  until that's comfortable  i would also practice in tempo  just two chords at a time  then moving on  [Music]  i need to make a note about the rhythm  which i consider to be 10 16 rather than  5 8 because  this is really divided into two halves  one  two  one  two  and if you count it in five that might  be confusing  all right so i hope you all can try at  least one or two or all three of these  versions let me know how it goes down in  the comments thank you so much for  watching and a special thank you to my  patrons over at patreon the bonus  material patrons will receive a link to  the pdf to these exercises  among other things  and i will see you all in the next video

